	
	1. Understanding Core Security Goals
		a. What is a use case?
			§ A goal that organization wants to achieve -> Verb-Noun format
			§ Same name across org, but can be used differently
			
		b. Ensure confidentiality
			§ Prevent unauthorized disclosure of data
			§ Best way is encrypting any kind of data e.g. PII, databases, data on mobile devices
			§ Access control help protect confidentiality by restricting the access to data
			§ Steganography supports confidentiality by hiding data
			
			i. Encryption
				Make data unreadable by unauthorized personnel. e.g.
				 PII data transmission
			ii. Access Controls
				Only authorizes personnel can access data
				1) Identification
					User Claim unique identity e.g. username
				2) Authentication
					Users Prove their identity with authentication e.g. passwords
				3) Authorization
					Grant or restrict access to data using authorization . e.g. permissions
			iii. Steganography and Obfuscation
				□ Steganography 
					® practice of hiding data within data
					® Can be used to support obfuscation
					® Hiding data in plain sight e.g. hidden msg in image by tampering few bits in file, if you look at file you won't notice anything but if other people know what to look for they can retrieve information
				□ Obfuscation  
					® Attempts to make something unclear or difficult to understand
					® Aka security by obscurity, not very reliable
		c. Provide integrity
			§ Provides assurances that data has not changed. ---> Integrity
			§ Ensuring no one has modified, changed or corrupted the data
			§ Integrity is lost when unauthorized user or unintended changes, malicious software cause data change.
			
			i. Hashing
				□ Number generated by implementing hashing algorithm against data(hash changes only when data changes)
				□ Verifies integrity
				□ Different MACs in security+
					® Media Access Control, physical address assigned to NIC
					® The Mandatory Access Control, one of access control method
					® Message authentication code, provides integrity similar to hash(sending MAC instead of hash)
				□ Lets one know if message has been changed but DOESN'T tell what exactly has changed
				□ e.g. web admin can calculate and post the hash of the file on website. Users can manually calculate the hash of the file after downloading the file and compare the hash.
				□ Manual errors can also be responsible for loss of data integrity e.g. faulty script to update bulk of database
				□ Different hashing algorithms include - MD5, SHA-1, and HMAC
			ii. Digital Signatures, Certificates and Non-Repudiation
				□ Similar in concept as that of handwritten signature
				□ Provides authentication preventing attackers from impersonating someone
				□ Provided NON-REPUDIATION, sender can't deny the fact that he/she sent the data with signature as digital signature will repudiate that claim
				□ AUDIT LOGS also provide the non-repudiation as it can log the record details such as who, when, what and where.
				□ Digital signature require the use of certificate and a Public Key Infrastructure(PKI). Certificated include keys used for encryption and the PKI provides the means to create, manage and distribute certificates.
		d. Increase Availability
			§ Data and services available when needed(commonly during organization office hrs at 8:00am to 5:00 om).
			§ Redundancy and fault tolerance methods ensure high levels of availability
			§ Patching the systems ensure that systems are up to date.
			i. Redundancy and fault tolerance
				□ Redundancy adds duplication to critical systems and provide fault tolerance.
				□ Goal is to remove each single point of failure (SPOF).SPOF fail --> system crash
				1) Disk redundancies
					Use of fault tolerant systems like RAID-1(Mirroring), RAID-5(Striping with priority) and RAID-10(striping with a mirror), allows system to continue even if a disk fails
				2) Server redundancies
					Failover clusters include servers and ensure service will continue to operate even if a server fails(service switches from failed server to operational server in same cluster).
					Virtualization increases server availability by reducing unplanned downtime 
				3) Load balancing
					Use of multiple servers to support a single service for high volume website
				4) Site redundancy
					The alternate site can be a hot site (ready and available 24/7), a cold site (a location where equipment, data, and personnel can be moved to when needed), or a warm site (a compromise between a hot site and cold site).
				5) Backups
					Backing up important data
				6) Alternate power
					Use of Uninterruptible Power Supplies(UPSs) and power generators
				7) Cooling systems
					Heating, ventilation and air conditioning(HVAC) reduce outages from overheating
			ii. Patching
				Organizations commonly implement patch to keep systems updated and reduce bugs in software
				A single problem-fixing piece of software for an operating system or application with reboot
			iii. Hot fix 
				A single problem-fixing piece of software for an operating system or application without requiring reboot
				
			iv. Categories of Updates
				1) Security Update
					Software code that is issued for a product-specific security-related vulnerability e.g. if security bug in MS-Word
				2) Critical Update
					Software code for specific problem addressing a critical, non-security bug in the software e.g. if Google chrome keeps crashing
				3) Service pack
					A tested, cumulative grouping of patches, hotfixes, security updates and possibly some feature or design changes. E.g. ion OS
				4) Windows Updates
					Recommended update to fix a non critical problem that users have found, as well as to provide additional features or capabilities. E.g. if Microsoft wanted to add new background images
				5) Driver update
					Updated device driver to fix a security issue or add a feature to a supported piece of hardware. E.g. update to network update.
					
		e. Resource versus security constraints
			§ Balance resource availability with security constraints. 
			§ e.g. selecting what confidential data to encrypt as encryption generally adds 40% size to data, Moreover it increases processing time which generally needs more power and can overheat system for encryption and decryption
			
	2. Introducing Basic Risk Concepts
		a. Risk
			§ Possibility or likelihood of a threat exploiting a vulnerability resulting in a loss.
			§ Risk Mitigation reduces chances of threat that will exploit vulnerability by implementing controls(aka counterguards and safeguards). Access controls with authentication and authorization help mitigate the risk
		b. Threat
			§ Any circumstance or event that has the potential to compromise confidentiality, integrity or availability of data and systems.
			§ Can result in Security Incident. 
			§ Includes intentional attacks, malicious software, accidental data loss and much more
			§ Can come from person inside or outside organization or such as natural disasters.
		c. Vulnerability
			It is a weakness, can hardware or software configuration.
	3. Understanding Control Types
		○ First three control types in list refer to how the security goals are implemented. The remaining control types refer to goals of the security control.
		a. Technical controls
			§ Use technology to reduce vulnerabilities
			i. Encryption  -------->protect confidentiality of data even over network, devices or on severs
			ii. Antivirus software --------> protection against malwares
			iii. Intrusion detection systems(IDSs) and intrusion prevention systems(IPSs) --> monitor network or host for intrusions and provide ongoing protection against various threats
			iv. Firewalls  ---> restricts network traffic
			v. Least privilege -----> individuals or processes are granted only the privileges they need to perform their assigned task or functions but o more
		b. Administrative Controls
			§ Use methods mandated by organizational policies or other guidelines.
			i. Risk assessment ------> help quantify and qualify risk within organization.
				1) Quantitative - use cost and asset values to quantify risk based on monetary values
				2) Qualitative - uses judgements to categorize risks based o probability and impact
			ii. Vulnerability assessment ---> attempts to discover current vulnerabilities 
			iii. Penetration tests ----> attempting to exploit vulnerabilities
			iv. Awareness and training -----> helps users maintain password security, understand threat 
			v. Configuration and change management  ---> configuration uses baselines to ensure that systems start in secure state. Change management ensures changes don't result in unintended configuration errors
			vi. Contingency planning  --> goal is to reduce the overall impact on the organization if an outage occurs
			vii. Media protection --->USB, flash drive, external and internal drives and backups tapes 
			viii. Physical and environmental protection -->physical control like cameras, door locks and hvac
		c. Physical Controls
			Any controls that you physically touch like lighting, signs, fences etc. many of these are also technical controls
		d. Control goals
			i. Preventive Controls
				Main goal is to try to have no security incident
				1) Hardening
					® Make system more secure than default configuration
					® Uses defense in depth strategy with layered security
					® Includes disabling unnecessary ports, implementing secure protocols etc.
					® Host hardening
					® Server hardening
					® Network device hardening
				2) Security awareness and training
					Ensure users are aware of threats and guide them to reduce vulnerabilities with training
				3) Security guards
					Guards prevent and deter many attacks
				4) Change management
					® Ensures changes don't result in unintended outages.
					® Instead of on the fly changes, they are submitted in advance and analyzed
				5) Account disablement policy
					Ensures user accounts are disabled when employee leaves, preventing anyone to use that account
			ii. Detective Controls
				□ In security incident attempt to detect when vulnerabilities have been exploited
				1) Log monitoring
					By monitoring different logs like firewall logs record, details about traffic that has been blocked can be found. Some automated methods of logs monitoring automatically detect the potential incidents and report them right after they have occurred.
				2) Trend analysis
					Log monitoring can also be done for to detect any trend e.g. IDS attempts to detect attacks and raise alerts. By analyzing past alerts, you can identify trends.
				3) Security audit
					Examines security posture of the organization e.g. password audit to detect if the password policy is ensuring the use of strong passwords.
				4) Video surveillance
					Can record activity and detect what occurred. Can also be used as deterrent control.
				5) Motion detection
					Detect motion from potential intruders and raise alarms
			iii. Comparing Detection and Prevention Controls
				□ Detective controls can't predict when incident will occur and it can't prevent it.
				□ Prevention controls stop the incident from occurring at all.
				□ e.g. video surveillance can detect whatever is going on is detective measure.
				□ e.g. guards are primarily preventive control
			iv. Corrective Controls
				Attempt to reverse the impact of an incident.
				1) IPS
					Attempts to prevent the attacks and then modify the environment to block the attack from continuing.
				2) Backups and system recovery
					Backups ensure that personnel can recover data if it is lost or corrupted. Also, system recovery procedures ensure administrators can restore the system after a failure.
			v. Deterrent Controls
				□ Attempt to discourage a threat like attacks or violating security policy.
				□ Many deterrent controls are preventive controls as well. e.g. security guards
				□ Following are the physical security controls
				1) Cable locks
					Securing laptops to furniture with cable lock deters thieves from stealing laptops.
				2) Hardware locks
					Other locks like locked doors securing a wiring closet or server room also deters attacks.
			vi. Compensating Controls
				□ Alternative controls used instead of a primary control. e.g. smart card for employees.
				□ However it may take some time for new employees to receive new card, to allow such employees Time-based One-Time Password(TOTP) can be implemented.
		e. Combining Control Types and goals
			Control types and control goals are mutually exclusive i.e. you can describe most controls using more than one category.
	4. Implementing virtualization
		Allows you to host one or more virtual systems or virtual machines(VMs) on single physical systems.
		It typically provides best return of investment(ROI) when organization has many underutilized servers.
		Provides increased availability with lower operational cost plus provides high level of flexibility when testing security controls, updates and patches as they can be easily revert back
			§ Hypervisor
				□ It is the software that creates, runs, and manages the VMs
				□ e.g. Vmware products, Microsoft Hyper-V products, and oracle VM Virtualbox
			§ Host
				□ The physical system hosting the VMs
				□ Requires more resources than typical system
				□ Although more resources cost more, it is still less expensive than paying for multiple physical systems
			§ Guest
				□ OS running on the host system are guests or guest machines
			§ Host elasticity and scalability
				□ Ability to resize computing capacity based on the load.
				□ e.g. with increase in traffic, you can increase the power and memory needed
		a. Comparing Hypervisors
			i. Type I hypervisors
				Run directly on the system hardware and often called bare-metal hypervisors as they don't need to run within an OS
			ii. Type II hypervisors
				Run as software within a host operating system.
				
						
				Above picture shows 3 guest OS using type II hypervisor based virtualization. Notice each guest has full OS including own kernel.
		b. Application Cell or Container Virtualization
			§ Runs services or applications within isolated application cells and does not have own kernel.
			§ It  uses fewer resources and is more efficient than a system using type II hypervisor virtualization
			§ Type I virtualization is faster and more efficient than type II virtualization.
			§ One drawback is that containers must use OS of the host e.g. if host is running Linux, all the containers must run Linux.
			§ Containers don't host entire OS, instead the host's OS and kernel run the services or app within each containers
			§ Live migration is when one VM is moved from one physical server to another physical server.
			§ As they are running in separate containers, none of the services can interfere with services and apps
			
						
			
		c. Secure Network Architecture
			VM can be part of secure network architecture. Main benefit of VM van be provide segregation, segmentation, and isolation of individual systems e.g. by disabling NIC in VM to prevent data transmission
			i. Snapshots
				□ Provides a copy of the VM at a moment in time to be used as backup
				□ Hypervisor keeps a record of all changes to the VM, if problem arises can go back to state of snapshot
				□ Administrators take snapshots of systems prior to performing risky operation
			ii. VDI/VDE and non-Persistence
				□ It is also possible to virtualize desktops.
				□  in virtual desktop infrastructure (VDI) or virtual desktop environment (VDE), user's desktop OS runs as VM on server.
				□ Main benefit if user PCs can have limited hardware resources. If PC can connect to a server over network , it can run a full-featured desktop operating system from server.
				□ Virtual desktops can support persistence or non-persistence. 
				□ In persistent VD, each user has custom desktop image which generally leads to more disk space required on server
				□ In non-persistent VD support same desktop for all users. Though user can make changes to desktop, it reverts to a known state when they log off.
			iii. VMs as Files
				□ VMs are just files just bit complex files
				□ As VMs are files, they can be easily moved from one server to another in case of overload.
				□ It is also easy to restore VM files in case of server failure as compared to physical host.
				□ Many virtualization products provide support to manage multiple virtual systems. e.g. you might have five physical servers hosting 3 servers each and you can manage all of them through a single management interface. This includes taking snapshots, reverting snapshots, and the virtual servers from one physical host to another.
			iv. Risks Associated with Virtualization
				1) VM Escape
					® It's an attack that allows an attacker to access the host system from within the virtual system.
					® In some cases, an attacker can run a code on the virtual system and interact with hypervisor.
					® As most virtual systems run on a physical server with elevated privileges, similar to admin privileges, it can be dangerous
					® When vendors discovers VM escape vulnerabilities, they write and release patches.
				2) VM Sprawl
					® Occurs when organization has many VMs that aren't managed properly.
					® Policies applicable to physical servers should also be applicable to VMs
					® If one of the vm is left without patch for some reason, it may be vulnerable to attacks.
					® Each vm adds additional load to physical server. If unauthorized vms are added to physical servers, they can consume system resources. The servers might become slower and potentially crash.
				3) Loss of confidentiality
					® As vm being just a type of files, it is also easy to steal
					® An disgruntled employee can copy vm, take it home and launch on physical server
			v. Elasticity
				§ Ability to Add more workstation or remove workstations  to virtualization environment is elasticity
			vi. Data remnants 
				Contents of virtual machines that exist as deleted files on a cloud-based server after deprovisioning of a virtual machine
