		c. Understanding Switches and Getting Help
			§ Windows command-line switch uses / or -.
			§ Linux  command-line switch typically uses -.
			§ In windows, help switch is identified with ?. e.g. ping /? Or ping -?, ipconfig /? Or ipconfig -?
			§ In linux, for help use pipe e.g. ping | help
		d. Understanding Case
			Most windows commands are case insensitive. Ping -6 localhoast = piNg -6 localHOST
			Linux commands are sensitive
		e. Ping
			§ Used to test connectivity, to test if can resolve name
			§ Checks connectivity by sending 4 ICMP echo request packets and wait for echo reply packet, if you receive echo reply packet then connectivity is working.
			§ Windows sends 4 ICMP echo requests
			§ Linux based systems continuously sends packets until ctr+c to stop
			i. Using Ping to check Name Resolution
				□ Typically DNS resolves name with IP addresses
				□ Some malware attempts to  break name resolution process for specific hosts. e.g. windows systems get updates from windows update server. In some cases, malware changes the name resolution process to prevent systems from reaching windows update server and getting updates.
				□ You can ping from any remote system and verify that name resolution is working.
			ii. Beware of Firewalls
				□ To avoid DoS attacks, firewalls commonly block ICMP traffic, as a result ping will fail even if remote system is operational
			iii. Using Ping to Check Security Posture
				□ You can also use ping to check the security posture of a network. For example, if you’ve configured firewalls and routers to block ping traffic, you can verify the firewalls and routers are blocking the traffic by using ping to check it.
				□ You can use ping to simulate an attack from a couple of computers to repeatedly send ping requests. If the IPS is working, it will block these attacks and the pings will stop receiving replies.
		f. Ipconfig, ifconfig, and ip
			§ Ipconfig =  internet protocol configuration, shows TCP/IP configuration information for system(includes ip, subnet, gateway, MAC, DNS) for all NICs
			§ Ifconfig = interface configuration, same as ipconfig
			§ Ipconfig /all = comprehensive listings of tcp/ip config info for each NIC
			§ Ipconfig /displaydns = shows contents of DNS cache
			§ Ipconfig /flushdns = erase contents of dns cache with this command
			§ Ifconfig eth0 = config of 1st ethernet NIC on linux system, similarly can be used with eth1,2,..and wlan0,1,2,…
			§ Ifconfig eth0 promisc = enables promiscuous mode on eth0. allows to process all the traffic it recieves.
			§ Ifconfig eth0 allmulti = enables multicast mode on NIC and process all the multicast traffic received by NIC
			§ Normally NIC uses non-promiscuous mode and only processes packets addressed directly ti its iP.
			§ Linux developers prefer "ip" command over ifconfig. But doesn't allow to use promiscuous mode
				• ip link show. Shows the interfaces along with some details on them
				• ip link set eth0 up. Enables a network interface
				• ip -s link. Shows statistics on the network interfaces
		g. Netstat
			§ Allows you to view statistics for tcp/ip protocol system, also gives ability to vies active tcp/ip network connection
			§ Many attacks establish connections from an infected computer to remote computer, with netstat you can identify these connections
			§ Netstat = listing of all open TCP connections
			§ Netsat -a = listing of all tcp and udp ports that a system is listening on in addition to all open connections
			§ Netstat -r = displays the routing table
			§ Netstat -s = displays details on network stats including how many bytes system sent and received
			§ Netstat -n = displays statistics of packets sent or received for specific protocols like IP, ICMP, UDP etc
			§ Netstat -n = displays addresses and port numbers in numerical order, useful if you are looking for information related to a specific IP or port
			§ Netstat -p protocol = shows statistics on specific protocol e.g. netstat -p tcp
			§ Can combine many of the netstat seitches e.g. netstat -anp tcp
				□ ESTABLISHED = normal state for data transfer phase of a connection, indicates active connection
				□ LISTEN = indicates system is waiting for connection request
				□ CLOSE_WAIT = indicates the system is waiting for a connection termination request
				□ TIME_WAIT = indicates system is waiting for enough time to pass to be sure the remote system received tcp based connection acknowledgement
				□ SYN_SENT = indicates system sent TCP SYN
				□ SYN_RECIEVED = indicates system sent TCP SYN-ACK, ACK handshake process.it is waiting for ack response. An excessive amount of SYN_RECIEVED states indicate SYN attack where attacker is flooding the with dyn packets but never finalizes the connection
		h. Tracert (Windows)/ Traceroute(Posix)
			§ Lists routes between two systems. (hop = routers)
			§ Traceroute for linux
			§ Identifies the IP and sometimes the hostnames of each hop in addition to round-trip times(RTTs)
			§ Used to identify faulty routers on network, where traffic stops(after ping fails) Network administrators typically use tracert to identify faulty routers on the network. Ping tells them if they can reach a distant server. If the ping fails, they can use tracert to identify where the traffic stops. Some of the hops will succeed, but at some point, tracert will identify where packets are lost, giving them insight into where the problem has occurred. Other times, they will see where the RTTs increase as traffic is routed around a faulty router. Tracing a path is especially valuable when troubleshooting issues through a wide area network (WAN).
			§ Tracert can verify traffic path in case traffic seems abnormally slow
